 ## Background
-The technique of using instancing to render large numbers of identical primitives, such as voxels, has been a standard optimization in real-time graphics for many years. Early implementations relied on display lists or vertex arrays to batch geometry, but these approaches suffered from CPU overhead when managing millions of instances [S2, S3, unofficial]. The introduction of hardware instancing in OpenGL 3.1 and subsequent APIs allowed the GPU to handle the repetition of primitives, significantly reducing the number of draw calls required [S2, unofficial].
-
-In the context of volume rendering, procedural instancing allows for the dynamic generation of voxel positions and attributes. For example, in fluid simulation rendering, instancing can be used to draw all slices of a voxelized fluid in a single draw call, using the instance ID to specify the target slice [S3, unofficial]. This approach is particularly effective when combined with stream output, which caches transformed vertices for deforming meshes, avoiding the need to recompute transformations for each slice [S3, unofficial].
-
-More recent developments have extended this concept to 3D Gaussian Splatting, where instancing is used to render millions of Gaussian primitives efficiently by computing their spatial extent and opacity in the shader [S1, unofficial]. Additionally, techniques like indirect draw calls allow the GPU to determine the number of instances to render based on data stored in buffers, further reducing CPU-GPU synchronization overhead [S1, S3, unofficial].
-
-The use of procedural noise to modulate instance attributes, such as density and displacement, has also been explored to create more organic and detailed volumetric appearances from simple surface primitives [S1, unofficial]. This method, while computationally intensive due to the high number of instances, provides a smooth visual result that is difficult to achieve with standard voxel rendering techniques.
+Instancing with Procedural/Shader Generation leverages the evolution of GPU hardware to shift geometric processing from the CPU to the GPU. Early voxel rendering relied on CPU-side generation of vertex buffers, which became a bottleneck as voxel counts increased. The introduction of hardware instancing in OpenGL 3.1 and Direct3D 10 allowed for the efficient rendering of multiple instances of the same geometry with minimal draw calls [S2, unofficial]. Subsequent advancements, such as indirect draw calls (Direct3D 11, OpenGL 4.4) and compute shaders, enabled the GPU to determine instance counts and parameters dynamically, further reducing CPU overhead [S1, S3, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in real-time fluid simulation visualization [S3, unofficial], terrain rendering [S2, unofficial], and Discrete Element Method (DEM) simulations [S4, unofficial].
 
